Write a MiniJinja (Jinja2-compatible) HTML template for the public website of {{ persona.company }} ({{ persona.industry.id }}). It must look like this company's own site: its own markup structure, class names and section names, not a known framework's demo. Plain semantic HTML; class names of your own invention; no external scripts.

The template receives these variables:
- home (bool): true on the front page
- heading (string): the page heading
- paragraphs (list of strings): body paragraphs
- links (list of objects with href, title, time): related pages; time is a unix timestamp
- nav (list of objects with href, label): site sections
- tagline (string): a one-line description, may be empty
- persona.company (string), site.login_path (string)
- epochs.cfg_anchor (unix timestamp, use for the copyright year)

Filters available besides the Jinja builtins: strftime(format) for timestamps, e.g. {{ "{{" }} link.time|strftime("%B %e, %Y") {{ "}}" }}. Output is HTML-escaped automatically.

Requirements:
- Start with <!DOCTYPE html>.
- Inside <head>, include exactly: {{ "{%" }} include "site/_head.html.j2" {{ "%}" }} (it writes the charset, viewport, title and stylesheet tags).
- Render heading, all paragraphs, all links (each linking to its href), all nav items, and a link to site.login_path.
- Use every variable only as described; do not invent others.
- Keep it between 40 and 120 lines.

